// [W-30 / F-3 + G-3] Task inspection (F-3) + minimal REPL (G-3).
//
// This file is the fixture for:
//   wlwl repl --file main.wll --serve 127.0.0.1:0 --eval :tasks
//
// One invocation covers all three readings at once:
//   (1) the REPL evaluates the program and prints its value;
//   (2) the read-only inspection endpoint starts on an EPHEMERAL loopback
//       port and proves itself with a loopback self-test;
//   (3) the `:tasks` meta-command prints the task snapshot as JSON.
//
// What the snapshot has to show:
//   * one task that finished            -> "state": "done"
//   * one task that was parked and then
//     force-cancelled at scope exit     -> "state": "cancelled"
//   * the task tree (scope tree with the tasks hanging on it)
//   * how long each task has been running -> "elapsed_ms"
//
// Why the parked task ends up "cancelled" and not "suspended":
//   * SPAWN is only legal inside a SCOPE (E0058, plan §10 D17) => every task
//     belongs to a scope.
//   * SCOPE exit hard-cancels every non-terminal child (concurrency book
//     §17.1: a child must not outlive its scope) => a task can NEVER be
//     observed as "suspended" AFTER the program ended.
//   * "suspended" (WHERE it is parked) is therefore a LIVE reading: it is
//     observable while the program is still running, through the endpoint.
//     The live path is guarded by the unit test in wlwl-eval (runtime.rs:
//     `the_inspect_slot_reports_the_suspend_point`) and by the manual
//     live check; this fixture pins the AFTER-the-run view.
//
// The channel is created OUTSIDE the SCOPE on purpose: a channel owned by
// the scope would be closed at scope exit, which would wake the parked task
// and make it finish with an ERR value (an orphan error report). Owned by
// the root scope, it stays open => the parked task is cancelled, not woken.

LET(ch, CHANNEL_NEW(0));

SCOPE(FUN(() ,
  // One task that finishes (the tree shows a terminal task next to the
  // cancelled one).
  LET(t_done, SPAWN(FUN(() , "done")));
  // One task that parks on the empty channel => the scope exit cancels it.
  LET(t_parked, SPAWN(FUN(() , CHANNEL_RECV(ch))));
  0
));

"p121-f3-g3-inspect-repl"
